# Module design

## Deep modules

Prefer **deep modules**: a lot of behaviour behind a small interface.

- Fewer functions with real behaviour beat many thin pass-through helpers.
- A module is shallow when its interface is nearly as complex as its
  implementation — a wrapper that only renames or forwards arguments adds cost,
  not value.
- **Deletion test**: if deleting a helper would just inline one line at each
  call site, delete it. If it would scatter real complexity across callers, it
  earns its place.
- Gesture handlers (`src/core/*/*.logic.ts`) stay thin; the math lives in the
  `*.utils.ts` helpers, where it can be unit tested.

## No speculative generality

Build for the requirements that exist, not the ones you imagine.

- **Every public prop is forever.** Do not add a prop, option or callback
  argument for a need nobody has; once published it is semver surface. Prefer a
  fixed, well-chosen constant (e.g. the rubber band's `0.55`) until a real
  request asks for a knob.
- No abstraction until there are at least two real call sites that want it.
- Don't scaffold empty folders or files "for later".

## Self-review smells

Before declaring work done, scan your own diff for these. Each is a judgement
call, not a hard violation — but if you leave one in, be able to say why.

- **Mysterious name** — a function, variable or type whose name doesn't say what
  it does. If no honest name comes, the design is murky.
- **Duplicated code** — the same logic shape in more than one place in the
  change (e.g. per-axis code copied for X and Y instead of one helper).
- **Data clumps** — the same few fields travelling together through signatures
  (`minX, maxX, minY, maxY, paddingX, paddingY`). Bundle them into one type.
- **Primitive obsession** — a raw number standing in for a concept (px vs % of
  the wrapper). Name it, or at least document its unit.
- **Speculative generality** — see above. Delete it.
- **Middle man** — a function that mostly delegates onward. Call the real
  target.
